      "content": "Answer the following question with a single MySQL SQL query against the current database. Explore the semantic network and verify with mysql_query as needed. The SELECT list must contain exactly the columns the question asks for, in the asked order. When the final SQL is verified, call the submit_SQL tool with it.\n\nThe following hints are provided to help you write the SQL:\n\n## Gold Tables\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT\n- SIS_SUBJECT_CODE\n- SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY\n\n## Column Mapping\n- subject code: SIS_SUBJECT_CODE.SUBJECT_CODE\n- subject code description: SIS_SUBJECT_CODE.SUBJECT_CODE_DESC\n- department name: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_NAME\n- school name: SIS_SUBJECT_CODE.SCHOOL_NAME\n- average department budget code: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPT_BUDGET_CODE\n- subject title: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.SUBJECT_TITLE\n- total units: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.TOTAL_UNITS\n- dense rank over the department code: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- number of subjects offered by the department: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY_KEY, SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- degree granting: SIS_DEPARTMENT.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING\n- Mathematics: SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_NAME\n\n## Join Keys\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT.SCHOOL_CODE <-> SIS_SUBJECT_CODE.SCHOOL_CODE\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE <-> SIS_SUBJECT_CODE.DEPARTMENT_CODE\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE <-> SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.OFFER_DEPT_CODE\n\n## Domain Knowledge\n- \"math\" is predicated by \"TABLE.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics'\"\n- \"Course 18\" is predicated by \"TABLE.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics'\"\n\n## Subquery Decomposition\n1. For each subject code where the department is degree granting and the department is Mathematics, show the subject code, subject code description, department name, school name, and the average department budget code within each school, ordered by department name.\n```sql\nSELECT t1.SUBJECT_CODE, t1.SUBJECT_CODE_DESC, t0.DEPARTMENT_NAME, t1.SCHOOL_NAME, AVG(t0.DEPT_BUDGET_CODE) OVER (PARTITION BY t1.SCHOOL_NAME ORDER BY t0.DEPARTMENT_NAME) AS avg_dept_budget_code FROM SIS_DEPARTMENT t0 JOIN SIS_SUBJECT_CODE t1 ON t0.SCHOOL_CODE = t1.SCHOOL_CODE WHERE t0.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING = 'Y' AND t0.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics' ORDER BY t0.DEPARTMENT_NAME\n```\n2. List the subject title, total units, department name, and school name for each subject offered, along with dense ranks over the department code ordered by department code, for subjects offered by departments that are degree granting, have a non-null subject code description, and are in the Mathematics department (Course 18).\n```sql\nSELECT s.SUBJECT_TITLE, s.TOTAL_UNITS, d.DEPARTMENT_NAME, d.SCHOOL_NAME, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY d.DEPARTMENT_CODE ORDER BY d.DEPARTMENT_CODE) AS window_column FROM SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY s JOIN SIS_DEPARTMENT d ON d.DEPARTMENT_CODE = s.OFFER_DEPT_CODE JOIN SIS_SUBJECT_CODE sc ON sc.DEPARTMENT_CODE = d.DEPARTMENT_CODE WHERE d.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING = 'Y' AND sc.SUBJECT_CODE_DESC IS NOT NULL AND d.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics'\n```\n3. For each subject code where the department is degree granting and the department is Mathematics, show the subject code, subject code description, department name, school name, and the average department budget code within each school, along with the subject title, total units, and dense rank over the department code ordered by department code for each subject offered by the Mathematics department, ordered by department name and dense rank.\n```sql\nWITH Subquery1 AS ( SELECT t1.SUBJECT_CODE, t1.SUBJECT_CODE_DESC, t0.DEPARTMENT_NAME, t1.SCHOOL_NAME, AVG(t0.DEPT_BUDGET_CODE) OVER (PARTITION BY t1.SCHOOL_NAME ORDER BY t0.DEPARTMENT_NAME) AS avg_dept_budget_code FROM SIS_DEPARTMENT t0 JOIN SIS_SUBJECT_CODE t1 ON t0.SCHOOL_CODE = t1.SCHOOL_CODE WHERE t0.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING = 'Y' AND t0.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics' ORDER BY t0.DEPARTMENT_NAME ), Subquery2 AS ( SELECT s.SUBJECT_TITLE, s.TOTAL_UNITS, d.DEPARTMENT_NAME, d.SCHOOL_NAME, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY d.DEPARTMENT_CODE ORDER BY d.DEPARTMENT_CODE) AS window_column, d.DEPARTMENT_CODE FROM SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY s JOIN SIS_DEPARTMENT d ON d.DEPARTMENT_CODE = s.OFFER_DEPT_CODE JOIN SIS_SUBJECT_CODE sc ON sc.DEPARTMENT_CODE = d.DEPARTMENT_CODE WHERE d.IS_DEGREE_GRANTING = 'Y' AND sc.SUBJECT_CODE_DESC IS NOT NULL AND d.DEPARTMENT_NAME = 'Mathematics' ) SELECT sq1.SUBJECT_CODE, sq1.SUBJECT_CODE_DESC, sq1.DEPARTMENT_NAME, sq1.SCHOOL_NAME, sq1.avg_dept_budget_code, sq2.SUBJECT_TITLE, sq2.TOTAL_UNITS, sq2.window_column FROM Subquery1 sq1 JOIN SIS_DEPARTMENT d ON sq1.DEPARTMENT_NAME = d.DEPARTMENT_NAME AND sq1.SCHOOL_NAME = d.SCHOOL_NAME JOIN Subquery2 sq2 ON d.DEPARTMENT_CODE = sq2.DEPARTMENT_CODE ORDER BY sq1.DEPARTMENT_NAME, sq2.window_column\n```\n\n## Question\nFor each subject code where the department is degree granting and the department is Mathematics, show the subject code, subject code description, department name, school name, and the average department budget code within each school, along with the subject title, total units, dense rank over the department code ordered by department code, and the number of subjects offered by the department, for each subject offered by the Mathematics department, ordered by department name and dense rank. Do not return any rounded answers."
